The original does a linear scan over every profile message on each call. This version builds a 65536-entry index table once and then answers each lookup in O(1).

- **Safe to build once:** the table is a function-local static built from the constant `mesgs` array. C++11 guarantees thread-safe initialization, and it does not depend on any mutable global state.
- **Same result for duplicates:** the table is filled from the last entry to the first, so if two entries share a `num`, the first one still wins, as in the linear scan.
- **Unknown numbers:** they hit the `0xFFFF` sentinel and return `NULL`, as before.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    static const std::vector<FIT_UINT16> table = []() {
        std::vector<FIT_UINT16> t(65536, (FIT_UINT16)0xFFFF);
        for (int i = MESGS - 1; i >= 0; i--)
            t[mesgs[i].num] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        return t;
    }();

    const FIT_UINT16 idx = table[num];
    if (idx == (FIT_UINT16)0xFFFF)
        return NULL;
    return &mesgs[idx];
}
```

